Z||000000||jaguiler||04-21-2001||06:17 PM||||I guess today was one of my worst days fixing the GT.  I read all the articles everything!  I was doing the front manifold.... and I could not even take off the air conditioning  compressor to do the work!!!!!  After 6 hours I gave up and put back the parts I managed to remove.... it does not seem like there is any space to take off the compressor....<BR>I think I also broke my oil pressure sensor sender....it had a plug on top which I unpluged so I could get to the upper bracket on my compressor.  I unpluged it and tryin to get a wrench in there to remove the bracket I broke one of the male plug ends coming from the sensor!  I managed to put that back together kind of half assed..... but I sitll have a leaking manifold!!!!  I just gave up on it....I guess I will live with the sewing machine...<BR>I am renaming my car to  "Singer"<P>My rear manifold came off with no problems...I assume my front one will be just as easy as soon as I get the F...ing compressor off!  Anyone in the maryland area experieced with this to give me some pointers?  Blakc88Gt? Anyone?  <P>------------------<BR>______<BR>__|aguiler||65.8.46.102||reg||1
Z||000001||Mark||04-21-2001||08:38 PM||||When I did mine, I removed the exhaust system from the wye pipe on back.  With the cat out of the way, there is alot more room  to work on the a/c compressor and front manifold.  This also made detailing the exhaust much easier.||||reg||1
Z||000002||jaguiler||04-21-2001||11:23 PM||||Well I have short new replacement cat...so I don't have too much problem there. It is the brackets on the top that gave me problems, ie wiring harness in the way...I am still steaming from my failed repaired atempt today...I spent about 8 hours and drove awya with the same problem I started wtih and a broken oil sensor.  Although I did take the opportunity to fix the wiring from my trunk solenoid.<P>------------------<BR>______<BR>__|aguiler||65.8.46.102||reg||1
Z||000003||voyagerspe||04-22-2001||12:00 AM||||I worked for about 5 hours on the compressor bolt when i did my engine swap(i didnt break the lines open so i didnt have to recharge).  I just couldnt get it loose.  Then a friend stopped by to ask how i was doing, I told him about my trouble with the compressor.  He simply said "well why dont you take the whole <BR>A/C bracket off the motor?"  10 minutes later i had the compressor off with the bracket.  I felt kind of dumb for not doing this sooner but im glad he came along or i might still be working on that bolt.||||reg||3
Z||000004||WKDFIRO||04-22-2001||12:12 AM||||The easiest way I have found to replace the front manifold is to unbolt the cradle at the back. (15 MM bolts near the exhuast tips) and let the cradle pivot down. The torque strut will keep it in the car. This is with the jacks supporting the car just forward of the cradle "hinges." <P>With the engine tilted forward and perhaps a wooden board to lay on across the engine you can get to the bolts (soak them in oil to lessen the chances of breaking them!) If you still need MORE space just unbolt the trunklid (I think 13 mm). To make sure that everything lines up when you put the trunk lid back on I used to get masking tape or duct tape and put it across the hinges. Then I make a cut and line up the tape when Im ready to put it back!<P>Let us know how you do when you go for round 2!||||reg||1
Z||000005||jaguiler||04-22-2001||12:17 AM||||I don't have a problem getting to the manifold bolts or getting my hands in there, after I remove the compressor.  The compressor of course is in the way of the bolts.  My main problem now is that I ca't get the compressor off.  I tried to take the bracket off the engine, (a big silver gray colored bracket, the upper one)  but I could not get a wrench in with out taking apart half my engine.....   I must be doing something wrong.<BR>I may have to wait till the Carlise show to ask advice or meet up with someone nearby...<P>Thanks for the responses though!<P>------------------<BR>______<BR>__|aguiler||65.8.46.102||reg||1
Z||000006||turbo86se||04-22-2001||11:31 AM||||When I lived in MD, the only person I took my Fiero to is Glenn Fogle. He lives in between Frederick and Hagerstown. He did all kinds of work to my old '85 Coupe. Very Reasonable prices. This was many years ago, but give him a try. I think this is his phone number: 301-293-1607.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=226077&a=1676320&p=20236791"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Rodney||04-22-2001||11:47 AM||||I removed and replaced my manifold in the car. Terribly hard to do. Spent many hours. Long extensions etc. I would look at dropping the cradle in the rear some next time!! You may have to unbolt the struts from the shock towers and watch the brake lines etc. Then work from the top. Should be fairly easy that way. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||WKDFIRO||04-22-2001||01:45 PM||||I don't remember having that much problem with the AC compressor, but it was a long time ago since I did it...<P>Good luck to you!<p>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 04-22-2001).]||||reg||5
